00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.910
To Ash and to Ash Raja Ramen and to Mike

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:22.740
to invite me to come here.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:24.210
It's been a great learning experience

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.433
listening to speakers this morning.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.540
And as Ted was talking about
what Yale has put together

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.110
here with Special Operations Command,

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:40.740
we have a similar relationship
at Carnegie Mellon

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.690
with the US Army.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.870
There's Army AI Center at Carnegie Mellon.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.870
The Army Futures Command
sends officers for education

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.540
at Carnegie Mellon in AI.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:59.160
And we also address senior
leadership one stars,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.590
two stars on data-driven leadership.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.350
And I can speak to that
as an important aspect

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:06.030
to the human capital aspects

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.123
associated with the discussion
that we've been having.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:13.200
So Ted talked about the AI stack.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.200
The AI stack is something
that Carnegie Mellon developed

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.890
to organize and frame the discussion

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:26.160
about the different
components of AI technology

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:30.690
that are relevant to how
it's gonna be deployed,

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:31.770
be it commercially,

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:32.760
be it in government,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.960
be it in to address the kinds of questions

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.153
that we are hearing about today.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.120
And it ranges, and it in some ways,

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.790
for those of you who are
very familiar with the ODA

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:48.270
or ODA stack, the observe, you know,

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:50.730
each of these different
elements of that stack,

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:53.850
the AI stack is sort
of organized similarly.

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:58.290
At the bottom layer is the
sensing or perception layer.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:02.730
And increasingly that
layer is in hardware.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:05.940
And this is about audio, video,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.453
multimodal text based perception.

00:07:11.175 --> 00:07:13.413
And those signals that are perceived,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:17.973
that's equals to the
observation, the ODA stack.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.390
And that perception then feeds

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:25.770
into a signal processing layer,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:26.940
which tries to make sense

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:30.150
of what's being actually perceived.

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:35.150
And then that then feeds
into this learning layer.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:38.040
And for a long, long time now,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.020
I mean there's been
tremendous developments

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:44.310
in the learning stack, which in itself,

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.490
as you know, has had
tremendous amount of progress,

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.220
particularly in the field of deep learning

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.143
and neural network architectures.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.670
Everybody's been excited
about what's happened

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:59.610
since the release of ChatGPT

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.340
and these so-called large language models.

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:05.700
They are all at the learning layer

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.260
of being able to take
vast amounts of corpora.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.590
So for instance, ChatGPT 4
was trained on 570 gigabytes

00:08:16.019 --> 00:08:19.350
of text and other kinds of corpora bit,

00:08:19.350 --> 00:08:24.350
code from GitHub, be it images
from Getty Images, video.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.870
So it's this immense amount of input

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:31.530
that's gone into the training
of these neural networks.

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.370
And these models have
grown in size as well,

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:40.370
the ChatGPT 4 has what's
called 170 billion parameters

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:41.940
in the neural network.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:44.100
So that learning layer

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.200
as the size of the training corpora

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.570
and the size of these
models have increased,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:52.140
the capacity and the
capabilities of these models

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.720
ranges from the kinds of capabilities

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.137
you've seen exhibited by ChatGPT.

00:08:58.137 --> 00:09:02.250
And ChatGPT is one of many
large language models.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:03.870
Google has something called Bard.

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:07.110
And there's lots of different
kinds of powerful models

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.780
that are being developed by organizations.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.230
I was at an emerging tech
event a couple of weeks ago,

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:17.220
and Adobe has its own large language model

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:18.900
for images and video.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.310
And increasingly you're seeing

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.250
the integration of these learning layers

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.230
and the output of those capabilities

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.600
in personal productivity tools,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:29.400
be it the Microsoft stack,

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:30.660
as Microsoft has announced,

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.410
or Adobe or Google Mail, et cetera.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.810
The learning stack then feeds into

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:39.098
a decision making layer.

00:09:39.098 --> 00:09:41.280
So what is it that's been learned?

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.540
How can it actually
support decision making

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:46.770
where you're using the patterns

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:48.360
that have been learned

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.240
on importantly even potentially
causally understanding

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.390
the relationships between
what's being observed

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.130
and what decision to take.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.990
And this is where it
could be a very useful

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.990
decision support device,
particularly to the human.

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.790
And this is the human, the loop,

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.440
but it could equally well potentially

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:09.630
support decision making autonomously,

00:10:09.630 --> 00:10:11.640
that's the decision making layer.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.650
The decision making
layer then on top of that

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.570
recedes the action layer.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.090
So the stack then at a very minimum,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.637
you could think of it as
having these four layers,

00:10:20.637 --> 00:10:22.860
the perception layer, the learning layer,

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.500
the decision making layer
and the action layer.

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.580
And these different layers can be deployed

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:31.140
via the enterprise.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:33.063
It can be deployed at the edge,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.840
conditional on the
constraints of the technology.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.140
Imagine a large language model.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.720
Today I don't think we have the devices

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.459
to actually deploy it
directly on a mobile device,

00:10:45.459 --> 00:10:49.860
because it takes immense amount
of compute and data power

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.530
to train these models, but once trained

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:56.190
could you imagine an image of these models

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:59.760
that could be put on a device,

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:02.070
that could be on a disconnected,

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.540
not connected to the network,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:06.810
not the way we use
ChatGPT today with an API,

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.420
but imagine it being on a
mobile device disconnected,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:11.220
that could potentially change the nature

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.820
of the deployment and the edge
for the kinds of use cases

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:17.343
that were being discussed earlier today.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:23.480
And the nature of these capabilities,

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.790
if you think of this is
what I've described entirely

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.400
is a technology stack

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:32.880
and an associated set of capabilities.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.640
The biggest challenges have been

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.360
how do you get people
trained with the skills

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:42.570
to be able to effectively
use this technology stack.

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:46.710
So just to give you an
example of something.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.080
We work like I said with the US Army,

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:50.970
they send us AI technicians,

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:53.040
there's technician training,

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.450
they are officers being trained

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.030
for being data engineers
or data scientists.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.340
And then the leadership has to understand

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.340
how best to put this
trained human capital to use

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.990
in the field using this technology.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.430
If you train these folks

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:09.660
and then put them back to work

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.150
doing exactly the same
old thing they did before

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.340
they did the training,
that's not much point.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.910
And therefore the futures
command training set,

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:19.410
if you think of this as a pyramid,

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:23.610
at the top end of the pyramid
are the PhDs and the masters,

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:25.077
then come a very large number of people

00:12:25.077 --> 00:12:26.940
who are gonna be deployers.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.590
And then there are lots of operators.

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:30.720
That's the AI technicians,

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.270
the officers being data
sciences and data engineers

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.460
plus people who are actually
gonna create the tech.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:38.280
So you need a very holistic view,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.340
and I think of this as
a human capital stack

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:44.010
that has to stand side by side

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.290
with the technology stack

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.870
to actually get the
most value out of this.

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:50.940
So let me stop here Ted,

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.100
and I'm sure there'll
be lots of opportunities

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:53.820
to build on this discussion.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:55.050
- All right, thank you so much.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.050
I'm gonna try to save
myself and all my questions

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.870
for just a minute 'cause
I wanna incorporate

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:03.120
some of the other expertise that we have.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.620
So just shifting from AI for
a moment to quantum computing,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:12.180
I was also struck by Mr. Innes's
comments about, you know,

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:16.950
just the challenge of grappling
with emerging technologies

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:20.430
with unclear time horizons and impact,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.440
and quantum computing strikes me

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.590
as an area where this is a
particularly acute challenge.

00:13:25.590 --> 00:13:29.550
So very grateful to
have Dr. Girvin with us.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.760
He's been a member of the
Yale faculty since 2001.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.190
He's the Eugene Higgins
professor of physics

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:37.020
and applied physics.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:40.740
He's had some significant
university administration roles

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:43.830
here including as deputy
provost for research,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:48.300
kind of overseeing Yale's
overall strategic planning

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:52.260
and resource allocation
and personnel decisions

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.350
with respect to the sciences across Yale.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:14:00.120
And he returned to full-time
teaching at Yale in 2017.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:01.890
But that's not all that he does.

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:05.070
He is of course himself
an exceptional scholar

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:08.790
in the theoretical studies of
quantum many particle systems.

00:14:08.790 --> 00:14:12.540
He's been part of setting up
the Yale Quantum Institute,

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:15.150
which is a really significant effort,

00:14:15.150 --> 00:14:17.400
which is sort of developing

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:18.900
a practical architecture

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:21.120
for construction of a quantum computer,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:25.680
which has one numerous
acclaim both nationally

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:27.540
as well as around the world.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.370
And in addition to his teaching,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:32.400
he was the founder and still is a member

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:36.570
of the Center for Quantum Advantage,

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:39.450
which is part of the
Brookhaven National Laboratory

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.790
and a Department of Energy funded effort

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:44.700
to try to harness a
better national approach

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.010
to understanding both the risks

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.330
as well as the opportunities

00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.190
that quantum computing might hold.

