00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:33.443
- Sure.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:35.973
Thanks very much Ted.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:38.820
So you guys have been throwing

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.950
a lot of acronyms at me today,

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.290
so I feel like I should start with

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.630
a little bit of vocabulary

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:48.090
and maybe some historical context

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.893
about what is this all about.

00:15:50.736 --> 00:15:55.736
So, you know, one of the
main pillars of physics

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.000
is classical mechanics.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:04.500
The theory of invented by Isaac Newton

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:09.480
more than 300 years ago that determines

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:12.333
the orbits of satellites and missiles,

00:16:13.665 --> 00:16:15.963
the orbit of the moon and so forth.

00:16:17.029 --> 00:16:20.193
It's fantastically successful theory,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.560
but it only applies to
sort of large objects.

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.630
Something goes wrong when you get down

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:32.100
to the scale of individual
atoms or individual electrons.

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.100
And we needed a new theory

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:38.340
beginning about a hundred years ago,

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.570
the quantum theory was invented

00:16:42.480 --> 00:16:45.180
and that led to what I'll call

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:47.490
the first quantum revolution

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:51.720
beginning a century ago that
brought us the transistor,

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:56.720
the laser, and the atomic
clock, which led to GPS.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:02.120
And the entire 20th century
technology revolution was built

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:06.453
on these first quantum devices,

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:11.520
but there's now a second
quantum revolution underway.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:14.310
It has huge promise,

00:17:14.310 --> 00:17:18.540
maybe a lot of hype for sure,

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.990
very uncertain timelines,

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:26.430
but it has the potential to be possibly

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.740
as big a revolution as
the one that brought us

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.200
the 20th century tech revolution.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:35.190
And it's based on not a
new theory of quantum,

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:38.430
but a new understanding of
this a hundred year old theory

00:17:38.430 --> 00:17:43.430
and the role of information
content in quantum states.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:48.780
And we now realize that
the transistor, the laser

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:52.410
and the atomic clock
while quantum machines,

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:55.290
they don't harness the full power

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:58.650
that is actually available
in quantum machines

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:00.183
and quantum technology.

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:05.490
And so the sort of three use cases

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:10.380
that are relevant to this
audience are quantum sensing.

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.020
It turns out in the quantum theory,

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.500
you have to think deeply
about what is a measurement,

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.080
what is detecting something.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:21.900
And it's more complicated and subtle

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:25.380
than in classical physics,

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:28.890
because when you measure
something it changes.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:30.930
But it's important to you

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:34.020
for the detection of
extremely weak signals.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:39.020
Small magnetic anomalies near
overhead above submarines,

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:44.523
gravitational anomalies
over caves and mountains,

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.693
weak radio signals, et cetera.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:53.490
The kind of quantum limited amplifiers

00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:58.490
that we build for our quantum
computers could easily,

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:01.050
they work in cell phone frequency ranges.

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:02.560
They can easily detect

00:19:03.719 --> 00:19:06.993
or perhaps even be overloaded
by a cell phone on the moon.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.450
The second is quantum
encryption and communication.

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:15.210
Current cryptographic protocols

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:19.830
are more or less uniformly
based on the difficulty

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:23.253
of some math problem,
solving some math problem.

00:19:24.540 --> 00:19:29.510
Quantum encryption security is based on

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.570
our inability to violate
the laws of physics.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.543
It's a different setup.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:40.440
And then finally, quantum computation.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:43.110
The ability to process information

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.980
in completely new ways

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.790
compared to traditional,

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:50.610
what I'll call classical computers.

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.030
It's a completely new
model of computation.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:59.030
Certain problems which
computer scientists classify

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:03.870
as difficult or exponentially hard

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:06.790
as you raise the size of some database

00:20:07.801 --> 00:20:10.420
are not necessarily exponentially hard

00:20:12.266 --> 00:20:15.123
if you can build a quantum computer.

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:19.800
Their advantages do not come from having

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:21.480
a faster clock speed.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.550
Often the clock speed, at least today,

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:27.900
is slower than the highly developed

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.450
rap fast classical
computers we have today.

00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:33.090
But it's a completely different model,

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:36.240
which for certain classes of problems

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:38.913
that can be solved more
readily on quantum hardware.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:41.763
So for example,

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:47.760
you only need a few thousand quantum bits

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:50.670
to do something extremely
interesting and powerful

00:20:50.670 --> 00:20:51.750
with a quantum computer.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.750
You don't need the kind
of scale of terabytes

00:20:57.450 --> 00:20:59.940
and things that you have
in today's computers.

00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.880
On the other hand, we
don't even have that yet.

00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:06.750
We're in a very early
stages of the technology.

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:11.160
The technology is imperfect,

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:12.963
makes lots of errors.

00:21:14.402 --> 00:21:17.310
The number of calculations we can do

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:20.550
or calculational steps we can do

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:24.213
before an error happens is very small.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.890
A few thousand perhaps today.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:31.170
So a grand challenge is to figure out

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:34.020
how to do error correction.

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:37.290
We also need to have a
greater understanding

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:42.030
of what kind of algorithms
can be invented,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:44.370
what kind of problems can be solved

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:45.900
more efficiently with this hardware.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:50.880
We understand something
about optimization problems

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:52.590
and simulation problems

00:21:52.590 --> 00:21:56.670
and machine learning
classification problems

00:21:56.670 --> 00:21:58.140
and things like that.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.360
But I wanna be very clear,

00:22:00.360 --> 00:22:04.440
I've heard a lot about big
data and high data rates

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:08.160
and things that probably big data problems

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.590
and machine learning problems,

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:13.620
while in principle there
are quantum advantages

00:22:13.620 --> 00:22:17.280
that will be the last area of application

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:19.930
for technical reasons that we need

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:22.860
for exponentially big data sets,

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:26.010
we actually need exponentially
many quantum bits

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:27.813
and we just don't have those yet.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:34.500
In terms of timeline quantum sensing

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:37.200
and quantum encryption are here now.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:41.250
I mean there are even companies
you can purchase from,

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.260
but with lots of limitations

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:48.247
in terms of bandwidth

00:22:48.247 --> 00:22:51.870
`and lack of being portable

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:54.180
or battle hardened and so forth.

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:57.480
But the technology is here.

00:22:57.480 --> 00:23:00.093
Computation, I don't know.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:04.203
I've been saying at least
10 years for a while now.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.550
Given that the people who
invented the transistor,

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:13.080
the laser and the atomic clock

00:23:13.080 --> 00:23:15.600
had no idea what they
were gonna be used for

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:19.683
I hesitate to say where this
is going or how quickly.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:25.140
And so I'm reasonably sure
they're gonna be, you know,

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:26.700
just as there were AI winters,

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:28.290
they're gonna be quantum winters.

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:30.540
