what their job is already
in the negative space.

00:28:50.580 --> 00:28:53.040
That's where all of the
opportunity and the risk is.

00:28:53.040 --> 00:28:56.460
So when I walk into a Fortune
500 company now and I say,

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:59.400
who here is in charge of
what we are not doing?

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:02.370
It doesn't compute because
people aren't thinking about

00:29:02.370 --> 00:29:03.630
that negative space.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:05.790
And so when you think
about creating a system,

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:08.880
how do you systematically
go after opportunities

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:11.490
and value creation in that negative space?

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:14.340
You know, for me, one of
the things that enjoyed

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:17.910
from Bill's presentation was
really the crystallization,

00:29:17.910 --> 00:29:20.160
and I'm gonna overly simplify here,

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:22.260
but if you think about the COCOMs,

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.330
they are driving outcomes.

00:29:24.330 --> 00:29:27.000
And when you think about
functional providers

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:29.610
like a SOCOM or any
sort of unit in the US,

00:29:29.610 --> 00:29:32.760
you are truly providing a capability.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:37.760
And so think about the world
when you do the AI pixie dust,

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:39.510
what's the outcome
you're trying to achieve

00:29:39.510 --> 00:29:41.820
or what's the function that
you're trying to maximize,

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:45.150
but be really crisp and
laser-like in your thinking around

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:46.530
what are you doing and why?

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:51.090
And so for me, I think that's
what really breaks down a lot

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:52.590
is the governance and the systemization

00:29:52.590 --> 00:29:54.210
of what you're doing.

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:56.760
'Cause VMware has 40,000 employees,

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:59.730
and so if we don't get the system right,

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:01.410
then we're gonna fail, and
so what does that mean?

00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.060
Go back to Bill's presentation.

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:06.000
And when you look at you
know, from satellites to subs

00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:08.400
and you think about the points in between

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:10.830
you are creating, there is a system there.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:14.250
And so sure any single node in that system

00:30:14.250 --> 00:30:16.890
is important, but way,
way, way more important

00:30:16.890 --> 00:30:19.320
is the architecture of that system.

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:21.510
And so if you think about architecture,

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.700
how does information flow back and forth

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:26.490
across a system so that any single node

00:30:26.490 --> 00:30:28.800
can underperform,

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:32.580
an individual fails but
the organization doesn't.

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:35.310
And so as we think about these great,

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.860
you know, quantum compute, AI, et cetera,

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:39.450
what to me is really important

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:41.820
is having the architecture right.

00:30:41.820 --> 00:30:42.827
And I'm a little biased because

00:30:42.827 --> 00:30:44.550
I mean at VMware and that's what we do.

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:47.340
But you think about how do you enable

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:51.090
an organization to have a
really clean application layer?

00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:53.760
How do you have a really clean data layer?

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.530
And you know, one of the things

00:30:55.530 --> 00:30:57.660
that I've just overseen
is the complete rebuild

00:30:57.660 --> 00:31:00.300
of our entire enterprise's
data foundation.

00:31:00.300 --> 00:31:02.280
We have structured and unstructured data.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:03.930
How do you get the most out of that?

00:31:03.930 --> 00:31:06.690
Well, you know, running a
transformation for a fortune,

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:09.840
you have Fortune 500 company,
it's not sexy to go say

00:31:09.840 --> 00:31:11.100
hi, we're gonna go fix the data

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:14.370
because your sales teams and your product,

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:15.690
you know, engineers, et cetera,

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:17.850
they don't see the oil
that is in the engine

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.290
and that makes the car go.

00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:21.480
And so what I really
would encourage people

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:24.360
to think about is really
the underlying system

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:26.790
because if you don't get
that foundation right,

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:28.800
then anything else is a capability

00:31:28.800 --> 00:31:31.800
whether it's OnPrem with
a hyperscaler on the edge,

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.110
sitting with an MSP, wherever you are,

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:37.650
that is really a function
of your foundation.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.810
So my point is, you know, always prepare

00:31:39.810 --> 00:31:42.240
for the thing that you can't see coming.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:44.883
And so when you're writing
requirements think about,

00:31:45.749 --> 00:31:47.160
to Ted's point, think about building

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:48.720
agility into your system

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:50.670
because by the time
something comes to life,

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:52.380
there will be a million more use cases

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:55.410
you didn't think of from when
you wrote the requirements.

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:58.470
And so did you create
something that is fluid

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:01.170
and functional that can be not just used

00:32:01.170 --> 00:32:02.490
for a point in time,

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.710
but to be used for, you know,

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:06.210
many, many years to come.

00:32:06.210 --> 00:32:08.070
So it's a relatively simple point,

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:09.750
but it's one that often gets missed.

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:11.730
