00:41:43.140 --> 00:41:44.313
It was on actually.

00:41:45.150 --> 00:41:45.983
Thank you Ted.

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:52.360
So two things to keep in
mind more broadly about AI

00:41:53.220 --> 00:41:56.550
is the technology, even
though from what you read,

00:41:56.550 --> 00:41:58.920
you might think it's some magic button

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:03.920
that will work perfectly all
the time in all situations,

00:42:04.620 --> 00:42:05.853
that's not the case.

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:11.880
It's really good
oftentimes on narrow tasks,

00:42:11.880 --> 00:42:15.810
that's where it's been shown
to be particularly good.

00:42:15.810 --> 00:42:19.350
And it's oftentimes brittle

00:42:19.350 --> 00:42:22.920
when you start deviating from the data

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:24.330
on which it was trained on.

00:42:24.330 --> 00:42:25.800
This is called drift.

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:30.630
There's this idea that
you train a model on data

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:32.580
and if what you're seeing in the world

00:42:32.580 --> 00:42:35.790
is not representative of
what it was trained on,

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:39.210
you might not get good
recommendations, good decisions.

00:42:39.210 --> 00:42:44.160
So one is to recognize that
robustness, brittleness,

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.640
these are things to be
really well educated about.

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:49.560
So it's almost like having

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:52.020
what these are often called
data cards or model cards

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:57.020
where for every AI there's a
need to have metadata about,

00:42:58.260 --> 00:43:03.260
you know, in what range is
this AI to be totally trusted,

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.070
what's the confidence
interval, if you will,

00:43:05.070 --> 00:43:08.520
around this AI that's sort
of an important aspect.

00:43:08.520 --> 00:43:11.220
Now, given the consequential
of the decision

00:43:11.220 --> 00:43:12.390
that you need to make,

00:43:12.390 --> 00:43:15.870
I mean, if it's about popping
an ad on somebody's computer,

00:43:15.870 --> 00:43:18.960
and if the AI made a
mistake in showing you an ad

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:22.020
that you're unhappy with,
that's not super consequential.

00:43:22.020 --> 00:43:22.853
But on the other hand,

00:43:22.853 --> 00:43:26.100
if it's a consequential
decision that has to do

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:27.150
with people's lives,

00:43:27.150 --> 00:43:29.130
or the kinds of services
you're providing them,

00:43:29.130 --> 00:43:32.070
or in even where people's
lives are at stake

00:43:32.070 --> 00:43:35.070
and the kinds of situations
that you all are engaged in

00:43:35.070 --> 00:43:39.370
those tasks the bar is very
high in terms of ensuring

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:45.530
are the values that we as a society,

00:43:45.690 --> 00:43:49.320
you as an organization have articulated.

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:51.660
And this is where the
responsible use part comes in.

00:43:51.660 --> 00:43:54.960
So you have accuracy,
effectiveness, efficiency,

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:59.010
you want the war fighter
to be better equipped

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:01.270
to be able to make these decisions

00:44:02.130 --> 00:44:04.200
and gain situational awareness,

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:06.690
be able to deal and digest data

00:44:06.690 --> 00:44:11.690
that's coming at a rate
that without the support

00:44:11.940 --> 00:44:12.900
they're not able to provide,

00:44:12.900 --> 00:44:15.510
All of that is on the
plus side of the column.

00:44:15.510 --> 00:44:17.970
On the negative side
of the column might be

00:44:17.970 --> 00:44:22.050
how do I trust what it is
that this thing is producing

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:27.050
and how do I ensure that what
it is that is producing is

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:30.540
a, been validated, verified, et cetera.

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.870
And two are there in the societal setting,

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:35.220
in domestic societal setting,

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:38.190
these are often referred
to in terms of, you know,

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:41.610
ensuring equity, or in this
case it might have to do

00:44:41.610 --> 00:44:44.297
with ensuring that the values

00:44:44.297 --> 00:44:49.297
that you have to sort of
balance are taken into account.

00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:50.970
So oftentimes, let me give you

00:44:50.970 --> 00:44:53.880
a societal example I'm familiar with.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:56.460
One might be asked to make a trade off.

00:44:56.460 --> 00:44:59.590
So oftentimes a trade off in
AI governance that arises is

00:45:02.201 --> 00:45:06.030
how much are you willing
to trade off on accuracy

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:08.760
to get an additional unit of equity?

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:11.550
And in your case you replace
equity with, you know,

00:45:11.550 --> 00:45:15.030
the appropriate dimension
that you are concerned about

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:18.840
or vice versa, can I get more of both?

00:45:18.840 --> 00:45:21.420
Can I be more accurate and more equitable,

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:23.520
or is there really a trade off?

00:45:23.520 --> 00:45:25.590
And if I'm gonna make that trade off,

00:45:25.590 --> 00:45:27.510
what are the weights that
people are gonna place

00:45:27.510 --> 00:45:29.760
on these things and who
comes up with these weights?

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:32.010
Is there guidance and doctrine

00:45:32.010 --> 00:45:33.300
that's gonna tell you

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:35.430
that this is how you're gonna
have to make these trade offs?

00:45:35.430 --> 00:45:39.810
So I think the high level
point that I wanna make

00:45:39.810 --> 00:45:43.680
is irrespective of whether
it's generative AI,

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:45.690
the ChatGPT like technology,

00:45:45.690 --> 00:45:48.210
which makes a lot of errors.

00:45:48.210 --> 00:45:49.620
It's not perfect by any means,

00:45:49.620 --> 00:45:52.548
but it does move the
needle on a number of tasks

00:45:52.548 --> 00:45:56.370
that you might be
interested in using it for,

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:58.920
so that's the accuracy or efficiency

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:01.170
or effectiveness under the dimension.

00:46:01.170 --> 00:46:05.490
You're always asking the
question by deploying this AI

00:46:05.490 --> 00:46:06.840
when can I trust it?

00:46:06.840 --> 00:46:09.900
So this is the part about
validity verification, et cetera.

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:12.000
And then when I actually use it,

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:13.290
how can I use it in a way

00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:16.200
where it's in aligned with my values?

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:18.450
And that's where this trade
off governance question

00:46:18.450 --> 00:46:21.930
comes up between improving
accuracy and efficiency

00:46:21.930 --> 00:46:23.670
and this equity point that I made.

00:46:23.670 --> 00:46:27.180
The larger point that you
oftentimes see firms making

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:29.613
this decision about
when they deploy AI is,

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:32.820
I think Mike made the point
that at the end of the day

00:46:32.820 --> 00:46:36.090
all of this is means to an
end it's absolutely true.

00:46:36.090 --> 00:46:38.580
Let me give you example from healthcare.

00:46:38.580 --> 00:46:41.340
In healthcare they take
the system as a whole,

00:46:41.340 --> 00:46:43.890
I don't care about whether
this algorithm is fair

00:46:43.890 --> 00:46:47.250
or accurate or whatever,
oftentimes it's a pipeline.

00:46:47.250 --> 00:46:50.370
I'm interested in the performance
of the system as a whole.

00:46:50.370 --> 00:46:51.750
And in healthcare there's something called

00:46:51.750 --> 00:46:52.890
the quintulpe aim.

00:46:52.890 --> 00:46:56.160
There are five dimensions
along which you rate

00:46:56.160 --> 00:46:57.813
any healthcare delivery system.

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:01.230
Is the patient doing better?

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:02.910
Is the provider doing better?

00:47:02.910 --> 00:47:04.773
Is the community doing better?

00:47:06.018 --> 00:47:09.720
And those dimensions, those
are called the quintuple aims

00:47:09.720 --> 00:47:11.880
of any healthcare system.

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:13.380
Might be useful to think about,

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:17.760
is there the equivalent of
a quintuple aim like that,

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:19.920
should there be something
that should guide,

00:47:19.920 --> 00:47:23.220
which is multiple
criteria that should guide

00:47:23.220 --> 00:47:27.150
the assessment and evaluation
of AI-based systems

00:47:27.150 --> 00:47:29.400
when AI is deployed in
the kinds of context

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:32.280
that you're working with.

00:47:32.280 --> 00:47:34.710
And responsible use comes down to

00:47:34.710 --> 00:47:36.930
how do I play with the parameters,

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:38.730
who's empowered to do that one?

00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:42.150
And two how can I assess
and evaluate the system

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:44.580
in terms of the objectives,

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:46.710
and the aims that matter to me most.

00:47:46.710 --> 00:47:48.060
So that's how I think about it.

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:49.410
- [Ted] Great, thank you so much.

00:47:49.410 --> 00:47:52.650
Steve. I think we're
still a little bit off

00:47:52.650 --> 00:47:56.970
from the use of quantum computing for life

00:47:56.970 --> 00:47:58.710
or death decision making of the sort

00:47:58.710 --> 00:48:02.640
that this audience is accustomed
to making on a daily basis.

00:48:02.640 --> 00:48:05.640
But you know, we are
still not that far away

00:48:05.640 --> 00:48:09.090
from being able to scope what
some of the risks might be.

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:10.890
And so just give us a sense of

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:13.290
what does concern you most

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:15.120
from the national security perspective?

00:48:15.120 --> 00:48:19.830
Is it the loss of secure communications?

00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:22.810
There's obviously a lot
of hyperbole surrounding

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:27.330
whether other nations
might develop in this area.

00:48:27.330 --> 00:48:31.080
China of course has a very
aggressive national quantum plan.

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:33.480
I'm curious if you have any thoughts on

00:48:33.480 --> 00:48:35.460
China's own activity in this space

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:39.300
that might be of relevance
to this group that's possibly

00:48:39.300 --> 00:48:43.350
really the only nation level
state capable of competing.

00:48:43.350 --> 00:48:47.190
Are the threats in your
mind sort of specific

00:48:47.190 --> 00:48:49.920
in terms of what some
of the impacts could be,

00:48:49.920 --> 00:48:52.290
or is it more this general fear

00:48:52.290 --> 00:48:54.840
of kind of a loss of
national competitiveness

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:58.140
as a new next generation technology

00:48:58.140 --> 00:49:00.483
enters the scene a decade from now?

00:49:01.380 --> 00:49:04.140
- Yeah, thanks Ted.

00:49:04.140 --> 00:49:09.123
So China is indeed investing heavily.

00:49:09.960 --> 00:49:13.860
It's very hard to say from the outside

00:49:13.860 --> 00:49:15.630
how efficiently et cetera.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:18.030
I mean lots of, just as in this country,

00:49:18.030 --> 00:49:20.880
many things have been relabeled quantum

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:22.470
to seek funding from the government.

00:49:22.470 --> 00:49:24.753
I know that's true in China.

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:28.623
But they're investing very heavily.

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:34.950
In terms of communication and encryption,

00:49:34.950 --> 00:49:38.460
there's sort of offense and defense

00:49:38.460 --> 00:49:42.453
and quantum contribute
to both sides in the end.

00:49:45.900 --> 00:49:48.250
If all of this can be made to work well

00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:54.480
privacy is enhanced
because there are forms

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:57.600
of quantum communication such that even

00:49:57.600 --> 00:50:01.260
an adversary with quantum capabilities

00:50:01.260 --> 00:50:05.613
will be unable to break the encryption.

00:50:06.449 --> 00:50:08.070
On the other hand,

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:12.900
a lot of current encryption
is based on the difficulty

00:50:12.900 --> 00:50:16.380
of solving certain mathematical problems

00:50:16.380 --> 00:50:19.590
without knowledge of a key

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:22.143
or other crib to get in.

00:50:23.507 --> 00:50:27.390
And it's perfectly possible
and presumably is going on

00:50:27.390 --> 00:50:32.010
that much of this encrypted
communication is currently being

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:33.810
captured and written to disks

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:38.810
and could be decrypted using
quantum hardware when...

00:50:40.530 --> 00:50:44.070
We're not close to being able
to apply shore's algorithm

00:50:44.070 --> 00:50:45.480
to do this, for example now,

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:50.480
but could happen within,
you know, 10 or 20 years.

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:52.800
So there's a lot of interest

00:50:52.800 --> 00:50:56.733
in what's called post quantum encryption.

00:50:58.200 --> 00:51:01.950
It's classical ordinary
mathematical encryption,

00:51:01.950 --> 00:51:04.680
but with the different
algorithms that are designed

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:06.870
to be difficult even
for quantum computers.

00:51:06.870 --> 00:51:11.100
So there's a lot of concerns,

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:13.590
I guess both on offense and defense

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:15.663
connected with this technology.

00:51:18.869 --> 00:51:22.590
The players in the world are China,

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:25.773
the US and a few countries in Europe,

00:51:26.670 --> 00:51:27.990
Switzerland, Germany,

00:51:27.990 --> 00:51:30.663
Austria, Sweden primarily.

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:37.410
And I think it's very important

00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:43.680
to take a long-term view of
sort of national investments

00:51:43.680 --> 00:51:48.390
in this just because
it's extremely uncertain.

00:51:48.390 --> 00:51:50.220
There isn't the guaranteed payoff,

00:51:50.220 --> 00:51:52.440
but we need to be,

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:54.450
you know, if there is a breakthrough,

00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:57.060
we need to have the workforce

00:51:57.060 --> 00:51:59.700
and the technical expertise and the people

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:04.203
and the ecosystem to
really respond to that.

00:52:05.460 --> 00:52:07.118
- Could I make one comment--
- [Ted] please, yes

00:52:07.118 --> 00:52:08.701
- by what Steve said.

00:52:08.701 --> 00:52:12.450
In all the things that I said
about responsible use of AI,

00:52:12.450 --> 00:52:15.300
one of the things to keep in
mind is that the adversary

00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:17.043
may not be responsible.

00:52:18.400 --> 00:52:21.600
So when you think about that setting,

00:52:21.600 --> 00:52:24.120
what would it take by way of technology,

00:52:24.120 --> 00:52:25.860
by way of human capital,

00:52:25.860 --> 00:52:28.200
by way of systems that we have,

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:31.380
that would allow for us
to compete and out-compete

00:52:31.380 --> 00:52:33.600
an adversary who might be irresponsible.

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:36.600
That's a good framing for
us to think about as well.

00:52:36.600 --> 00:52:37.740
- [Girvin] That's very good.

00:52:37.740 --> 00:52:38.820
- [Ted] Thank you.

00:52:38.820 --> 00:52:40.260
Mike I wanted to get your thoughts

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:43.830
on one other area that
Mr. Janice touched on,

00:52:43.830 --> 00:52:46.080
which was cybersecurity

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:49.200
and supply chain security in particular.

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:54.200
Obviously managing such a vast
cloud computing enterprise

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:58.860
as you do, you know, the
attack surface is vast, right?

00:52:58.860 --> 00:53:03.810
The adversaries seem to
be growing in capability,

00:53:03.810 --> 00:53:05.610
diffusing in capability

00:53:05.610 --> 00:53:07.800
from the most sophisticated state actors

00:53:07.800 --> 00:53:11.730
to a wide range of kind of gray actors

00:53:11.730 --> 00:53:14.280
or even non-state actors in this space.

00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:18.030
So emerging technologies including AI

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:20.580
of course have the ability both to

00:53:20.580 --> 00:53:24.120
accelerate the cybersecurity concerns,

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:27.930
but also maybe hold out some
opportunities for detection

00:53:27.930 --> 00:53:30.390
and mitigation and building resilience.

00:53:30.390 --> 00:53:32.700
And just kind of curious how you grapple

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:35.760
with this challenge
from your vantage point

00:53:35.760 --> 00:53:38.580
in sort of both the risk
as well as the benefit.

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:40.020
- Ted, it's a great question.

00:53:40.020 --> 00:53:41.610
Like you heard, I spent some time in DC

00:53:41.610 --> 00:53:43.380
so I'm really good at
not asking the question.

00:53:43.380 --> 00:53:45.000
I was not answering the
question I was asked,

00:53:45.000 --> 00:53:47.460
but the question that I wish I was asked,

00:53:47.460 --> 00:53:48.750
we'll come back to the cyber thing.

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:50.850
I think there's an elemental
point to what you're saying

00:53:50.850 --> 00:53:52.560
that is universally applicable to whether

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:55.117
it's cyber or any other technology.

00:53:55.117 --> 00:53:59.490
You know, if you think back
when the bicycle was invented,

00:53:59.490 --> 00:54:01.230
parents got really nervous that their kids

00:54:01.230 --> 00:54:03.090
were gonna be able to go too far away

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:04.950
and they were losing control.

00:54:04.950 --> 00:54:06.960
You know, most of us in this room remember

00:54:06.960 --> 00:54:09.210
we started navigating
when we learned to drive

00:54:09.210 --> 00:54:12.210
with Rand McNally maps
and now we're on Waze.

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:13.560
If it's map and compass,

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:16.140
I was a silver ranger and grid square guy

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:17.280
and now everybody else is using

00:54:17.280 --> 00:54:20.160
these little handheld GPSs instantly.

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:21.510
You know we always have

00:54:21.510 --> 00:54:24.360
a little bit of nostalgia for the past,

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:27.210
at the same time you can't
live without the present.

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:28.410
And so if you think about

00:54:28.410 --> 00:54:32.220
the universal truths of
technology advancement,

00:54:32.220 --> 00:54:33.930
there's always a little bit of,

00:54:33.930 --> 00:54:36.390
oh no, we can't let go of the past,

00:54:36.390 --> 00:54:37.980
but we can't live without the present.

00:54:37.980 --> 00:54:40.740
And so that's a really
important thing to remember

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:42.390
that as we're talking about models,

00:54:42.390 --> 00:54:45.450
to Peter's point earlier around like,

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:47.340
the previous discussion around

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:48.990
when will the computers
be doing everything

00:54:48.990 --> 00:54:52.860
and we just be watching,
you know, that is coming.

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:55.080
If you really think
about what are we doing,

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:56.970
think about the world
in three simple layers.

00:54:56.970 --> 00:54:59.310
You've got data, you've got logic,

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:03.090
and then you've got an
opinion or a decision.

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:05.820
And so you very crisply
need to separate your data

00:55:05.820 --> 00:55:06.810
and your logic and your opinion,

00:55:06.810 --> 00:55:09.750
and all you're really doing mostly is

00:55:09.750 --> 00:55:13.260
unpacking logic and digitizing it.

00:55:13.260 --> 00:55:14.310
You know when I was the commander

00:55:14.310 --> 00:55:16.680
of special operations task
force southeast, 2000,

00:55:16.680 --> 00:55:17.980
people running everything

00:55:19.049 --> 00:55:20.883
in southeastern Afghanistan in 2012.

00:55:22.020 --> 00:55:26.220
I personally was responsible
for 1100 times clearing,

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:29.040
you know, 500 pound JDMs from aircraft

00:55:29.040 --> 00:55:30.540
to drop on a ground.

00:55:30.540 --> 00:55:34.227
So now all I'm doing is
a decision making system.

00:55:34.227 --> 00:55:36.390
And so if you really think about it,

00:55:36.390 --> 00:55:38.490
all I'm doing is unpacking logic

00:55:38.490 --> 00:55:41.370
of when to say yes and
when to say no, that is...

00:55:41.370 --> 00:55:43.700
Dr. Krishnan and Dr.
Girvin could program that

00:55:43.700 --> 00:55:44.850
in a split second.

00:55:44.850 --> 00:55:47.280
All we need to do is get
from like what's happening,

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:49.750
not just in my brain, but
in my unit's collective

00:55:50.640 --> 00:55:52.680
brain and getting that down.

00:55:52.680 --> 00:55:54.510
That is very replicable.

00:55:54.510 --> 00:55:58.350
Now the thing is with model
drift to Dr. Krishnan's point,

00:55:58.350 --> 00:56:00.570
you have to be very careful
because at some point

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:02.160
there will be wrong decisions.

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:03.740
And so when there...

00:56:05.040 --> 00:56:06.570
We won't be defined whether or not

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:08.100
there are wrong decisions,

00:56:08.100 --> 00:56:11.520
we will be defined by how we
handle the wrong decisions.

00:56:11.520 --> 00:56:13.230
That's the most critical piece.

00:56:13.230 --> 00:56:15.687
Just like if we ever did
something wrong with a,

00:56:15.687 --> 00:56:17.820
and I'm proud to say we
never harmed anybody,

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:20.370
we shouldn't harm on those
1100 air to ground drops.

00:56:20.370 --> 00:56:22.200
But let's just say we did, you know,

00:56:22.200 --> 00:56:24.450
how do you diagnose down to the root cause

00:56:24.450 --> 00:56:25.920
what the failure was?

00:56:25.920 --> 00:56:27.780
That feedback is not different

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:30.210
than the product that
we're developing at VMware.

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:33.390
There's a product for
VMware's for our products,

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:35.160
excuse me, a market for our products.

00:56:35.160 --> 00:56:36.390
There's product market fit.

00:56:36.390 --> 00:56:38.490
You have gaps in between where we are

00:56:38.490 --> 00:56:39.720
and where we wanna be for the market.

00:56:39.720 --> 00:56:41.670
And then, oh, by the way,
the market's always moving.

00:56:41.670 --> 00:56:43.500
How do we systematically get after that?

00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:45.000
Well, sometimes we're wrong.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:46.290
A lot of times we're wrong.

00:56:46.290 --> 00:56:49.440
But as long as we're right
more than we're wrong,

00:56:49.440 --> 00:56:51.330
that's okay, will we be defined

00:56:51.330 --> 00:56:52.740
with how we handle the wrongs?

00:56:52.740 --> 00:56:55.020
The wrongs have to feed
back into that loop.

00:56:55.020 --> 00:56:57.150
So with cyber, look, we're gonna have,

00:56:57.150 --> 00:57:00.630
I ran two and a half billion dollar PNL

00:57:00.630 --> 00:57:02.520
for financial services at Cognizant

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:04.233
before being at VMware.

00:57:05.580 --> 00:57:06.690
We had a ransomware attack

00:57:06.690 --> 00:57:08.820
where everything was shut down.

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:13.800
And when UBS is calculating
their end of day PNLs

00:57:13.800 --> 00:57:15.900
at every desk and then
rolling it up to the PNL

00:57:15.900 --> 00:57:17.880
for the whole firm and saying,

00:57:17.880 --> 00:57:20.430
what are the margin calls
that we need to make

00:57:20.430 --> 00:57:22.560
because there were some
bad, you know, somebody,

00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:24.990
some other customers or
clients made bad trades.

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:26.880
Like that's a risk decision

00:57:26.880 --> 00:57:29.220
that's really critical for a big bank.

00:57:29.220 --> 00:57:30.990
So if we get that wrong,

00:57:30.990 --> 00:57:33.060
how do we make sure that we enable

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:34.710
the learning that goes with that.

00:57:34.710 --> 00:57:38.910
So with cyber we have things go wrong.

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:40.680
How do we get to the root
cause of what happened

00:57:40.680 --> 00:57:42.750
and simultaneously be pressed forward?

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:43.680
One other quick point,

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:47.730
and I absolutely infinitely
respect the VC community

00:57:47.730 --> 00:57:50.070
and the comments that Sam made.

00:57:50.070 --> 00:57:53.163
I would also just offer a
counter and a flip side to it.

00:57:54.330 --> 00:57:57.090
Even in the previous, there
was a question around,

00:57:57.090 --> 00:57:59.040
you know, VC, we hold up
the word in the military,

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:01.830
we just kind of munged
VC with private equity.

00:58:01.830 --> 00:58:03.820
Be careful because the VC world

00:58:04.967 --> 00:58:07.170
is not usually enterprise grade software.

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:09.330
I'm a huge proponent of feeding that

00:58:09.330 --> 00:58:10.500
into the Department of Defense,

00:58:10.500 --> 00:58:12.270
the national security
community, et cetera.

00:58:12.270 --> 00:58:13.500
We have to have that.

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:17.220
However come you don't often
find SOC2 compliant software

00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:18.540
in the VC space.

00:58:18.540 --> 00:58:21.630
And so as you look to proliferate

00:58:21.630 --> 00:58:24.150
where we need to go as a
national security community,

00:58:24.150 --> 00:58:25.980
you have to make sure you connect back

00:58:25.980 --> 00:58:27.750
to enterprise grade software

00:58:27.750 --> 00:58:29.550
that's going to be able
to do well at scale

00:58:29.550 --> 00:58:31.920
and it's not different
than the cyber firms Ted.

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:34.353
- Well, I mean to your point, you know,

00:58:35.970 --> 00:58:39.005
there needs to be accountability, right?

00:58:39.005 --> 00:58:42.630
If DIU vendor is developing a product

00:58:42.630 --> 00:58:47.040
that SOCOM is buying and it doesn't work

00:58:47.040 --> 00:58:50.100
as we heard in the previous session,

00:58:50.100 --> 00:58:53.050
there needs to be accountability
for that with that vendor.

00:58:54.000 --> 00:58:57.110
That's where the procurement policy making

00:58:57.110 --> 00:58:59.550
is so crucial that because these are

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:03.660
nat sec level type of
threats that we're facing,

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:06.960
and if that product
were to fail with a unit

00:59:06.960 --> 00:59:11.760
in a deployed, in a foreign
enemy territory, wow.

00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:12.593
That kind of thing.

00:59:12.593 --> 00:59:15.540
And to your point, VCs are absolutely,

00:59:15.540 --> 00:59:18.000
they're looking at first go-to-market,

00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:18.930
et cetera, that kind of thing.

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:21.780
And they're not taking their
cutting corners in a way.

00:59:21.780 --> 00:59:23.970
But that's why, you know,

00:59:23.970 --> 00:59:27.540
the technical evaluation
board needs to be just as

00:59:27.540 --> 00:59:30.600
where I have to say ATL is leading

00:59:30.600 --> 00:59:32.430
in a lot of ways with procurement

00:59:32.430 --> 00:59:35.040
and devising new mechanisms

00:59:35.040 --> 00:59:39.660
to provide that best technical
value to the war fighter

00:59:39.660 --> 00:59:41.250
and holding folks accountable too.

00:59:41.250 --> 00:59:44.340
And they're doing that by
bringing in industry experts

00:59:44.340 --> 00:59:49.340
to actually examine that product
that it, they're, you know,

00:59:49.670 --> 00:59:51.330
as a third party, not them,

00:59:51.330 --> 00:59:53.250
they're bringing in some
professor and saying,

00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:55.047
hey sir, can you analyze this?

00:59:55.047 --> 00:59:58.080
- And can I jump on that?
- Yeah, please.

00:59:58.080 --> 00:59:59.610
- If I'm in Bill's role,

00:59:59.610 --> 01:00:01.530
the thing that I really
wanna make sure is happening

01:00:01.530 --> 01:00:03.480
is you have two types of testing

01:00:03.480 --> 01:00:04.980
before you roll something out.

01:00:04.980 --> 01:00:07.020
There's quality engineering, QE testing,

01:00:07.020 --> 01:00:08.580
making sure is the engineering

01:00:08.580 --> 01:00:09.930
have you run all the combinations

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:12.030
and permutations of what can go wrong.

01:00:12.030 --> 01:00:13.740
And that is often short stroked

01:00:13.740 --> 01:00:15.360
in any product company.

01:00:15.360 --> 01:00:17.250
'Cause you get compressed
and you're like late

01:00:17.250 --> 01:00:18.360
and that's the last step and you're like,

01:00:18.360 --> 01:00:19.440
oh my gosh, how can we do

01:00:19.440 --> 01:00:21.690
what we're supposed to do in
four weeks in just a week?

01:00:21.690 --> 01:00:23.730
I do that just to be clear,

01:00:23.730 --> 01:00:25.170
and I get pushed against the wall

01:00:25.170 --> 01:00:27.120
and sometimes I need to
make a hard decision.

01:00:27.120 --> 01:00:29.460
Do we miss our deadline or do we say,

01:00:29.460 --> 01:00:30.840
nope, we're gonna test for four weeks.

01:00:30.840 --> 01:00:31.770
Sometimes I choose one,

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:33.120
sometimes I choose the other.

01:00:33.120 --> 01:00:35.280
But the other part of it
that often gets overlooked

01:00:35.280 --> 01:00:37.770
is UAT, user acceptance testing.

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:39.450
And so going back to your customers,

01:00:39.450 --> 01:00:40.920
your clients and saying,

01:00:40.920 --> 01:00:43.140
all right, before we
really harden this product,

01:00:43.140 --> 01:00:47.070
we want your feedback from
a consumer perspective.

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:48.720
And don't forget the DoD,

01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:50.190
the national security community

01:00:50.190 --> 01:00:55.190
is a consumer that can drive
the development of products.

01:00:55.260 --> 01:00:57.060
- And field testing too.

01:00:57.060 --> 01:00:58.650
Where is it going to be deployed?

01:00:58.650 --> 01:01:02.040
Will that product work
in that deployment area?

01:01:02.040 --> 01:01:06.123
That is not happening
and we'll get better.

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:09.420
- [Ted] Let's open it up to questions

01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:10.983
and thoughts from the audience.

01:01:20.190 --> 01:01:22.217
- Come on, it's about my coat kerchief.

01:01:22.217 --> 01:01:25.217
(audience laughing)

01:01:26.910 --> 01:01:28.967
- [Audience Member] What is
AI dusty, can I have some.

01:01:28.967 --> 01:01:31.967
(audience laughing)

01:01:34.110 --> 01:01:35.010
- [Audience Member] I'll ask a question.

01:01:35.010 --> 01:01:36.651
- [Ted] Oh, we'll get both of you.

01:01:36.651 --> 01:01:37.484
Go ahead. Yeah.

01:01:37.484 --> 01:01:38.400
- [Audience Member] I
was just gonna ask Mike,

01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:40.170
can you say anything
more about the project

01:01:40.170 --> 01:01:42.930
that you led that involved the structured

01:01:42.930 --> 01:01:44.250
and unstructured data,

01:01:44.250 --> 01:01:45.990
and reconceiving that

01:01:45.990 --> 01:01:48.040
or restructuring that across the company?

01:01:49.650 --> 01:01:52.964
- I can, and I'll be quick
and glad to take it offline.

01:01:52.964 --> 01:01:55.200
The VMware is a 25 year old enterprise.

01:01:55.200 --> 01:01:56.760
What happens for...

01:01:56.760 --> 01:01:58.110
I'll talk about VMware,

01:01:58.110 --> 01:02:00.360
but it's also metaphorically
any large enterprise,

01:02:00.360 --> 01:02:01.590
I don't care if you're Amazon, Google,

01:02:01.590 --> 01:02:02.423
Microsoft, whatever,

01:02:02.423 --> 01:02:04.140
you have tangled knots

01:02:04.140 --> 01:02:06.420
sometimes in these enterprises that are,

01:02:06.420 --> 01:02:09.210
have just been created
because number one is

01:02:09.210 --> 01:02:10.950
success can create an ability

01:02:10.950 --> 01:02:13.380
to just go buy the next
thing and layer it on.

01:02:13.380 --> 01:02:16.350
And the proverbial spaghetti plate

01:02:16.350 --> 01:02:18.600
just gets more and more
larger and more tangled.

01:02:18.600 --> 01:02:21.120
And so for us, we never stepped back

01:02:21.120 --> 01:02:25.890
and actually like really
created the governance

01:02:25.890 --> 01:02:27.060
around our data.

01:02:27.060 --> 01:02:29.010
And we had poor hierarchy,

01:02:29.010 --> 01:02:32.040
meaning what lives where in what ecosystem

01:02:32.040 --> 01:02:33.920
and how do you get after that?

01:02:33.920 --> 01:02:36.690
And so what we operate.

01:02:36.690 --> 01:02:38.820
Number one is we created
a vision for a new world.

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:40.380
We ran on the old world.

01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:42.480
And then there's a lot of
tension in any organization

01:02:42.480 --> 01:02:44.190
to say, why aren't you
paying attention to me

01:02:44.190 --> 01:02:46.830
'cause I need more
paperclips and chewing gum

01:02:46.830 --> 01:02:48.750
to go run the old stuff.

01:02:48.750 --> 01:02:49.860
You have to swing...

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:52.080
I have limited, you know,

01:02:52.080 --> 01:02:54.360
a finite amount of resources people

01:02:54.360 --> 01:02:57.750
to either run the company
or transform the company.

01:02:57.750 --> 01:02:59.070
So I need to make a decision.

01:02:59.070 --> 01:03:01.320
How much allocation do I spend toward run

01:03:01.320 --> 01:03:02.610
and toward transform.

01:03:02.610 --> 01:03:05.520
The simple math problem the
more you put on transform,

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:06.510
the faster you're gonna do it,

01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:09.120
but the louder the complaints
are gonna be around the run.

01:03:09.120 --> 01:03:10.800
And so, you know, I
tried to get that right

01:03:10.800 --> 01:03:12.360
over the last couple years.

01:03:12.360 --> 01:03:14.220
Sometimes I got it right,
sometimes I got it wrong.

01:03:14.220 --> 01:03:17.010
But where we popped out on the other side

01:03:17.010 --> 01:03:19.350
was a modern data architecture

01:03:19.350 --> 01:03:22.320
with incredible governance and hierarchy

01:03:22.320 --> 01:03:25.350
so that now we can really benefit

01:03:25.350 --> 01:03:28.020
from the things that Dr.
Krishnan is talking about

01:03:28.020 --> 01:03:31.470
and sitting on top of models
that can say, you know,

01:03:31.470 --> 01:03:33.780
look at what's a customer's
propensity to buy.

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:36.120
They already have product number A,

01:03:36.120 --> 01:03:38.910
are they gonna buy product C, F or L?

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:40.140
And these are the kinds of things

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:44.610
that can ultimately help us
drive more top line revenue

01:03:44.610 --> 01:03:47.010
or save costs and therefore
improve the bottom line.

01:03:47.010 --> 01:03:47.970
But why is that important

01:03:47.970 --> 01:03:49.800
for the national security community?

01:03:49.800 --> 01:03:52.500
It's because think about the
world in a GDP framework.

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:54.780
Remember it's just
labor times productivity

01:03:54.780 --> 01:03:57.960
equals the total economic
output of the world.

01:03:57.960 --> 01:03:59.010
You know, there are a lot of us in here

01:03:59.010 --> 01:04:00.800
with incredible pasts and you know,

01:04:00.800 --> 01:04:02.340
at the end of the day though

01:04:02.340 --> 01:04:04.080
we are largely commodities

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:06.090
where our government can go buy more of us

01:04:06.090 --> 01:04:07.530
if they have more money.

01:04:07.530 --> 01:04:09.330
A lot of us are very
expensive commodities,

01:04:09.330 --> 01:04:10.590
but we're commodities nonetheless.

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:13.740
And so more GDP equals more capacity

01:04:13.740 --> 01:04:15.900
for our nation to create more, you know,

01:04:15.900 --> 01:04:18.030
special people like in this room,

01:04:18.030 --> 01:04:20.580
or to pay down debt or
to buy more healthcare,

01:04:20.580 --> 01:04:22.470
more education, more whatever.

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:24.870
And so driving productivity for the nation

01:04:24.870 --> 01:04:27.540
really is job number one.

01:04:27.540 --> 01:04:29.520
- Just make a quick comment to follow up.

01:04:29.520 --> 01:04:31.860
One of the biggest challenges
with the structured

01:04:31.860 --> 01:04:33.240
and unstructured data is,

01:04:33.240 --> 01:04:35.280
traditionally our view is

01:04:35.280 --> 01:04:39.750
this data is being used
to train models to,

01:04:39.750 --> 01:04:42.840
for "good purposes".

01:04:42.840 --> 01:04:47.250
But these data can also
be subject to manipulation

01:04:47.250 --> 01:04:51.090
and become a type of a vector for attack.

01:04:51.090 --> 01:04:53.520
So you could have an adversary change

01:04:53.520 --> 01:04:56.040
or change the nature of your corpus,

01:04:56.040 --> 01:04:57.720
or inject stuff into your corpus

01:04:57.720 --> 01:05:00.810
that then gets your model
to behave differently

01:05:00.810 --> 01:05:01.860
than you would want it to.

01:05:01.860 --> 01:05:04.380
Or the model itself is something that

01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:06.780
is subject to potential attack.

01:05:06.780 --> 01:05:10.680
So that's why I think the
system's view of the problem

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:11.910
is really important to take

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:14.940
rather than just look at one
algorithm or what the data is,

01:05:14.940 --> 01:05:17.760
you need to go all the way
from problem formulation

01:05:17.760 --> 01:05:19.770
to data, to the methods,

01:05:19.770 --> 01:05:22.350
all the way out to what
comes out on the other side.

01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:23.460
- Can I ask you a question?

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:24.293
Is that legal?

01:05:24.293 --> 01:05:25.456
- Of course.

01:05:25.456 --> 01:05:27.270
- How do you know when it's broken down?

01:05:27.270 --> 01:05:32.270
- So the big question here
is when you have this,

01:05:32.859 --> 01:05:33.692
this is where you have to have,

01:05:33.692 --> 01:05:35.880
if you have ground truth,

01:05:35.880 --> 01:05:38.910
meaning you know, in some
cases what the ground truth is,

01:05:38.910 --> 01:05:41.250
then the question is
when there is variance

01:05:41.250 --> 01:05:43.020
is there a way in which you can check back

01:05:43.020 --> 01:05:46.140
as to why the model is not
working like it's supposed to?

01:05:46.140 --> 01:05:48.210
In the case where you
don't have ground truth,

01:05:48.210 --> 01:05:51.450
this becomes a hugely challenging problem.

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:53.910
But this data contamination problem,

01:05:53.910 --> 01:05:55.650
which is the problem we are talking about,

01:05:55.650 --> 01:05:57.240
where you have an adversary attacking

01:05:57.240 --> 01:05:59.490
the data sets that you're training on,

01:05:59.490 --> 01:06:02.700
itself is something that
you can bootstrap your AI

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:04.213
around and ask the question

01:06:04.213 --> 01:06:05.910
and is the data contaminated or not?

01:06:05.910 --> 01:06:07.760
It doesn't have to be a binary thing,

01:06:09.030 --> 01:06:11.640
that's itself is a subjective,

01:06:11.640 --> 01:06:13.080
it's a topic of study.

01:06:13.080 --> 01:06:17.580
- Well, I mean data manipulation
is so crucial these days

01:06:17.580 --> 01:06:20.940
just because in the outside
world we're seeing the rise of

01:06:20.940 --> 01:06:23.280
deep fakes and everything
that's happening right now,

01:06:23.280 --> 01:06:27.930
and tools are so important
to effectively corroborate

01:06:27.930 --> 01:06:31.050
that report if that is happening
or this is a fake report

01:06:31.050 --> 01:06:32.850
or is this a real report?

01:06:32.850 --> 01:06:36.900
What is happening and what
are the actual core sources

01:06:36.900 --> 01:06:38.550
that it's being reported from?

01:06:38.550 --> 01:06:41.100
Because there's so much
haziness and fuzziness

01:06:41.100 --> 01:06:43.170
that's happening on
the news and what's on.

01:06:43.170 --> 01:06:44.947
My dad is on Facebook and he is like,

01:06:44.947 --> 01:06:46.020
"Did you see this report?"

01:06:46.020 --> 01:06:47.880
I'm like, "Dad, no. Stop".

01:06:47.880 --> 01:06:49.380
You know, it's just one of those things

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:52.830
because there's so much
misinformation being pumped

01:06:52.830 --> 01:06:56.790
by state actors but also
commercial actors as well too.

01:06:56.790 --> 01:06:58.947
So that is something we need be cognizant.

01:06:58.947 --> 01:07:00.750
- The challenge that Sam speaks about

01:07:00.750 --> 01:07:03.600
I think is when you have
this type of misinformation

01:07:03.600 --> 01:07:05.190
getting pumped into the system,

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:08.430
and that gets then added
back into the corpus.

01:07:08.430 --> 01:07:10.980
Then you get the training of these models

01:07:10.980 --> 01:07:13.510
being affected by what's been added in.

01:07:13.510 --> 01:07:17.220
- Well, like the hallucination of ChatGPT.

01:07:17.220 --> 01:07:18.690
- That could be for altogether different.

01:07:18.690 --> 01:07:19.944
- Sure.

01:07:19.944 --> 01:07:20.959
(both laughing)

01:07:20.959 --> 01:07:21.792
It hallucinates for others.

01:07:21.792 --> 01:07:22.980
- Yeah. But ChatGPT is just,

01:07:22.980 --> 01:07:27.980
it's putting a response back
in a very nice language,

01:07:28.680 --> 01:07:32.340
but it's also taking rument
as well that's out there

01:07:32.340 --> 01:07:34.590
from forums, et cetera, whatever,

01:07:34.590 --> 01:07:37.860
and saying, hey, this is
actually what's going on.

01:07:37.860 --> 01:07:39.210
No, that is not,

01:07:39.210 --> 01:07:40.800
because right now in the bottom

01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:43.020
when that prompt does appear,

01:07:43.020 --> 01:07:45.450
you don't see any sources, right?

01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:48.570
But we're gonna see ChatGPT also evolve.

01:07:48.570 --> 01:07:51.030
Right now it's text space
where you're reading it

01:07:51.030 --> 01:07:52.650
and you're analyzing it,

01:07:52.650 --> 01:07:54.930
but in like six months
or a year or whatever,

01:07:54.930 --> 01:07:58.380
you're gonna see a full
video production of...

01:07:58.380 --> 01:08:02.220
I was joking earlier, professor
Girvin's, six years of,

01:08:02.220 --> 01:08:06.937
you know, lectures all
condensed into one hour

01:08:07.890 --> 01:08:12.120
with a AI developed Professor Girvin.

01:08:12.120 --> 01:08:13.590
So it is just one of those things.

01:08:13.590 --> 01:08:16.380
It's going to be revolutionary,

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:19.170
but there also needs
to be some safeguards,

01:08:19.170 --> 01:08:20.820
guardrails around it.

01:08:20.820 --> 01:08:22.890
And I know some countries are doing that.

01:08:22.890 --> 01:08:24.780
I mean some countries have already,

01:08:24.780 --> 01:08:27.990
Italy I think was just ban ChatGPT.

01:08:27.990 --> 01:08:29.340
But it's just, you know,

01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:32.130
that's where policy needs to catch up.

01:08:32.130 --> 01:08:33.600
- [Ted] Thank you. There
was one other question here

01:08:33.600 --> 01:08:35.790
and then we'll get to wrap up.

01:08:35.790 --> 01:08:37.590
- [Dave] Hi Dave Jewel from J Seven.

01:08:38.610 --> 01:08:39.960
Bear with me for just a minute.

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:42.120
So a lot of the things you
came up comes into mind

01:08:42.120 --> 01:08:45.480
as an old planner, as an
educator, as a researcher.

01:08:45.480 --> 01:08:49.230
And the secretary brought up
the morning about war gaming

01:08:49.230 --> 01:08:53.640
in the department usually starts
after deterrence has failed

01:08:53.640 --> 01:08:56.430
where sauce value is not the same

01:08:56.430 --> 01:08:58.443
as we propose it is prior to that.

01:09:00.900 --> 01:09:04.380
Done hundreds of types
of war games in business.

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:06.420
Most of them that we use in the work,

01:09:06.420 --> 01:09:08.370
most of them are based
on correlation of forces

01:09:08.370 --> 01:09:12.810
and means some sort of attrition
model of combat forces.

01:09:12.810 --> 01:09:16.200
So that clear data, clear logic,

01:09:16.200 --> 01:09:20.553
so we can get to Mike's
opinions based on good research.

01:09:21.510 --> 01:09:26.510
In the conflict, the contest for influence

01:09:26.850 --> 01:09:29.910
in spaces such as we were
talking about disinformation,

01:09:29.910 --> 01:09:33.420
so MISO or PSYOPs information
support operations

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:36.633
or civil affairs or
expeditionary medicine.

01:09:38.010 --> 01:09:39.927
Is anybody doing this 'cause I don't know.

01:09:39.927 --> 01:09:43.800
And if so, how can we tap
into it using the tools of AI

01:09:43.800 --> 01:09:46.980
and machine learning to
collect the data we have

01:09:46.980 --> 01:09:48.150
to include at some level

01:09:48.150 --> 01:09:52.420
the excursion into some
percentage that is misinformation

01:09:53.430 --> 01:09:57.120
to apply the logic with those parameters

01:09:57.120 --> 01:09:58.920
that the decision makers set

01:09:58.920 --> 01:10:03.330
so you can reduce the
ambiguity to a decision.

01:10:03.330 --> 01:10:06.600
One movement that I'm
watching with fascination now

01:10:06.600 --> 01:10:09.570
is a lot of bespoke war gaming

01:10:09.570 --> 01:10:12.330
in war gaming communities attributed with

01:10:12.330 --> 01:10:14.580
some of our PME institutions everything.

01:10:14.580 --> 01:10:16.050
And they're fascinating,

01:10:16.050 --> 01:10:19.083
but they operate almost
entirely in the opinion world.

01:10:20.490 --> 01:10:22.710
War game designer makes rules,

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:25.290
makes a good presentation of it,

01:10:25.290 --> 01:10:29.250
and thinkers go in and
bring their experience

01:10:29.250 --> 01:10:30.780
and their wisdom to it,

01:10:30.780 --> 01:10:32.250
and come out with what they do.

01:10:32.250 --> 01:10:34.700
We learn lessons from that
for military planning.

01:10:35.850 --> 01:10:39.000
But how can we use AI to take what is a

01:10:39.000 --> 01:10:42.000
combat attrition model in conflict

01:10:42.000 --> 01:10:43.500
like the old movie "War Games"

01:10:44.940 --> 01:10:48.180
and apply it to in the
space to the left of

01:10:48.180 --> 01:10:50.715
battle for influence with partners

01:10:50.715 --> 01:10:52.620
and against adversaries.

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:55.680
- So right now you asked,
the first thing was that,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:57.510
are we doing this right now?

01:10:57.510 --> 01:10:59.970
The office of net
assessments in the Pentagon,

01:10:59.970 --> 01:11:01.053
they are doing this.

01:11:01.891 --> 01:11:03.690
They have a pilot program
that they're working with

01:11:03.690 --> 01:11:05.380
IARPA and DARPA right now

01:11:06.570 --> 01:11:09.090
that it's being developed
in such a way where

01:11:09.090 --> 01:11:11.640
it's examining all the war gamings

01:11:11.640 --> 01:11:14.700
that have happened with
academic institutions,

01:11:14.700 --> 01:11:16.260
Columbia, et cetera.

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:18.960
And they're compiling all of that data

01:11:18.960 --> 01:11:21.750
and seeing how countries
have reacted in the past

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:23.970
and how they will react with the current

01:11:23.970 --> 01:11:26.367
core technology capability they may have,

01:11:26.367 --> 01:11:27.900
and what the investments are.

01:11:27.900 --> 01:11:29.790
It's still in pilot phase right now,

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:30.990
but I'll get with you offline

01:11:30.990 --> 01:11:32.550
and share some additional data with you.

01:11:32.550 --> 01:11:33.632
- [Dave] Thank you sir.

01:11:33.632 --> 01:11:34.650
That's in the office of net assessment

01:11:34.650 --> 01:11:36.840
that's not over the J Seven world

01:11:36.840 --> 01:11:38.100
of the more traditional folks.

01:11:38.100 --> 01:11:41.250
Yeah. Great. I'm excited
and interested in that.

01:11:41.250 --> 01:11:42.420
I was hoping you answer--

01:11:42.420 --> 01:11:43.590
- [Ted] Dr. Krishnan final thoughts.

01:11:43.590 --> 01:11:47.070
Any ideas on use of AI for simulations,

01:11:47.070 --> 01:11:50.130
war games or maybe complex decision making

01:11:50.130 --> 01:11:52.830
that might occur in the future

01:11:52.830 --> 01:11:55.470
that might help us think
about strategic challenges?

01:11:55.470 --> 01:11:59.223
- Absolutely. Including the
context that you raised.

01:12:02.168 --> 01:12:06.240
So conditional on having crisp definitions

01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:08.130
of what misinformation is.

01:12:08.130 --> 01:12:10.440
Because what's misinformation
to you might be just,

01:12:10.440 --> 01:12:12.303
you know, good information to me.

01:12:13.140 --> 01:12:15.150
So as long as we have crisp definitions

01:12:15.150 --> 01:12:18.360
of what misinformation is, then I think

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:21.630
the challenging issue really is

01:12:21.630 --> 01:12:25.230
that we now have technology
in particular again

01:12:25.230 --> 01:12:27.180
using these large language models

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:31.860
that currently have been
created and made popular.

01:12:31.860 --> 01:12:33.510
They have two elements to them.

01:12:33.510 --> 01:12:35.460
The first part is what's
called a transformer.

01:12:35.460 --> 01:12:38.700
The second part's what's
called a adaptive fine tuner.

01:12:38.700 --> 01:12:42.120
The adaptive fine tuner is
designed usually to take bias

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:44.670
and toxicity out of the language model.

01:12:44.670 --> 01:12:48.270
But that same technique
can be used to amp up

01:12:48.270 --> 01:12:50.070
the kinds of messages I could send

01:12:50.070 --> 01:12:52.860
to a populace to manipulate them

01:12:52.860 --> 01:12:54.810
to get max influence over them.

01:12:54.810 --> 01:12:57.330
In other words, I create
different messages,

01:12:57.330 --> 01:12:59.970
run it by humans and figure out

01:12:59.970 --> 01:13:03.630
which messages have the most
capacity to influence somebody.

01:13:03.630 --> 01:13:07.530
So what we then have is a machinery then

01:13:07.530 --> 01:13:11.790
that has economies of scale and scope

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:15.240
in terms of being able to
send out over a network.

01:13:15.240 --> 01:13:17.070
So if you control some kind of a,

01:13:17.070 --> 01:13:20.400
so if you take TikTok or a Facebook

01:13:20.400 --> 01:13:22.410
or an Instagram or any of these kinds

01:13:22.410 --> 01:13:24.030
of social media platforms,

01:13:24.030 --> 01:13:26.820
you have the capacity to
flood these with messages.

01:13:26.820 --> 01:13:28.110
And it doesn't have to be just text.

01:13:28.110 --> 01:13:30.210
It could be to Sam's
point, it could be audio,

01:13:30.210 --> 01:13:31.350
it could be video.

01:13:31.350 --> 01:13:34.950
And I think we have a very
interesting case study coming up.

01:13:34.950 --> 01:13:36.900
I think I was in a earlier presentation,

01:13:36.900 --> 01:13:39.330
where seems to be multiple
elections coming up

01:13:39.330 --> 01:13:41.380
in multiple countries, including our own.

01:13:42.390 --> 01:13:46.680
So I think you're gonna see
a very significant amping up

01:13:46.680 --> 01:13:49.800
of the extent of misinformation

01:13:49.800 --> 01:13:52.710
and disinformation being
deployed on the internet.

01:13:52.710 --> 01:13:54.540
And I think it behooves us all,

01:13:54.540 --> 01:13:55.620
I think to do three things.

01:13:55.620 --> 01:13:57.420
I think one of course is, you know,

01:13:57.420 --> 01:14:00.420
better technology to try and identify

01:14:00.420 --> 01:14:05.070
and classify and take out and
weed out this misinformation.

01:14:05.070 --> 01:14:07.454
The question is who's liable for it?

01:14:07.454 --> 01:14:10.050
You know, where does the
incentive like to remove that?

01:14:10.050 --> 01:14:10.980
That's one.

01:14:10.980 --> 01:14:15.423
The second I think has to
do with educating people to,

01:14:16.410 --> 01:14:18.270
as Sam was pointing out,

01:14:18.270 --> 01:14:19.980
don't believe everything
what you see on the internet.

01:14:19.980 --> 01:14:22.473
That's a long run kind of effort.

01:14:23.400 --> 01:14:25.990
And then the third is
being able to sort of

01:14:27.060 --> 01:14:28.743
take these types of, you know,

01:14:29.730 --> 01:14:32.370
this is gonna be core to our democracy.

01:14:32.370 --> 01:14:34.470
So do you think about content labeling?

01:14:34.470 --> 01:14:36.270
Just like when you buy wine

01:14:36.270 --> 01:14:40.380
you know it comes from
California or from Italy.

01:14:40.380 --> 01:14:43.080
Should content that
comes from an AI engine

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:45.210
be actually watermarked?

01:14:45.210 --> 01:14:47.880
Should it require content labeling?

01:14:47.880 --> 01:14:49.410
Should there be peer evaluation?

01:14:49.410 --> 01:14:50.243
You know what I mean?

01:14:50.243 --> 01:14:52.443
Wine spectator says that bottle is a 93

01:14:52.443 --> 01:14:54.270
and that bottle is an 89.

01:14:54.270 --> 01:14:57.030
So should you have peer
reviews of content?

01:14:57.030 --> 01:14:59.220
So there are lots of
different policy approaches

01:14:59.220 --> 01:15:01.770
one could think about as
well in addition to this,

01:15:01.770 --> 01:15:03.720
but it's a hugely complex problem

01:15:03.720 --> 01:15:05.790
and I don't think
there's an easy solution.

01:15:05.790 --> 01:15:08.130
And we've been dealing with this now for,

01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:10.500
it's getting close to a decade

01:15:10.500 --> 01:15:13.440
and we don't have really good
purely technical solutions

01:15:13.440 --> 01:15:15.660
because this is a socio-technical problem,

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:17.550
it's not a technical problem alone.

01:15:17.550 --> 01:15:19.710
So any solution's gonna have to be

01:15:19.710 --> 01:15:21.180
a sociotechnical solution.

01:15:21.180 --> 01:15:22.320
- [Ted] Absolutely.

01:15:22.320 --> 01:15:24.017
Please join me in thanking our panelists.

01:15:24.017 --> 01:15:26.202
(audience applauding)

01:15:26.202 --> 01:15:28.785
(subtle music)

